output:
 ```json
{
  "review": "I'm surprised over the number of folks that have rated this entry as their favorite 'Chan'. Didn't they ever see 'Chan at the Opera' or 'Chan at Treasure Island?'--- the latter ironically written by John Larkin, who dropped the ball here. This plot is a train wreck and overloaded with pointless characters. First, viewers are required to recall the sordid details of Steve McBirney's (played by venerable thug and HUAC squealer Marc Lawrence) 1929's murder spree. Let's not forget he escaped a capital murder rap at the courthouse with a lone policeman on his tail. There's also a victim that was fished out of a river 11 years earlier that no one ever seems concerned about. Then there's the suspension of disbelief required when all the characters are seemingly trapped in the wax museum (although Inspector O'Matthews manages to wield his fat wet rear end inside through a window). Why is Joan Valerie (as Cream's assistant) in this movie? She can't even handle pliers properly--- I realize Chan suffers the same boo-boo but yeesh, he's 66 years old here-- (and has less than 10 lines--- and her character's motivation is too weak to ever be adequately 'splained (excuse me, when I'm on a rant I write like Ricky Ricardo). The Mary Bolton (Marguerite Chapman) character is written to as a eager wide-eyed moron, apparently existing only for the vapid romantic interest of horndog lawyer Tom Agnew (played by the ferret-faced Ted Osborne). Why is Willie Fern a character? Why couldn't McBirney's henchman pulled the switch at 8:20? (not a spoiler, okay?!). One wonders how, with the IQ of lint he manages to dress himself or why he hadn't stepped in front of a bus years ago. Toler himself is given a little more acting rope than usual (a plus) and the real kudos go to set designer Thomas Little and cinematographer Virgil Miller who created some genuinely spooky atmosphere... but this entry has less logic than a Ritz Brothers film. I'm still boggled by how a toothpick can be used as a blow gun.",
  "translate": "Sono stupefatto per il numero di persone che hanno votato questa entrata come la loro preferita 'Chan'. Non hanno mai visto 'Chan all'Opera' o 'Chan sull'Isola della Tesoro?'--- quest'ultima scritta da John Larkin, che ha sbagliato qui. Questa trama è un disastro in fiamme e caricata di personaggi inutili. Prima, gli spettatori sono obbligati a ricordare i dettagli sordidi del caso Steve McBirney (interpretato dal vecchio mafioso e denunciatore HUAC Marc Lawrence) del 1929. Non dimentichiamo che ha evitato il processo per omicidio capitale al tribunale con una sola polizia in coda. C'è anche un vittima che è stata pescata fuori dal fiume 11 anni fa, che nessuno mai sembra preoccuparsi. Poi c'è la sospensione della disinvoltura richiesta quando tutti i personaggi sembrano intrappolati nel museo di cera (tuttavia Inspector O'Matthews riesce a mettere in mostra il suo grosso posteriore umido attraverso una finestra). Perché è Joan Valerie (interpretata come l'assistente di Cream) in questo film? Non può nemmeno maneggiare le forbici corretto--- capisco che Chan ha lo stesso problema ma yeesh, ha 66 anni qui-- (e ha meno di 10 righe--- e il suo motivo personale è troppo debole per essere mai adeguatamente spiegato (mi scuso, quando sono in una rissa io scrivo come Ricky Ricardo). Il personaggio della Mary Bolton (Marguerite Chapman) è stato scritto come una curiosa ragazza innocua, apparsa sembrere solo per interesse romantico vapido di avvocato Tom Agnew (interpretato dal volpe-volto Ted Osborne). Perché è Willie Fern un personaggio? Perché il braccio destro di McBirney non ha potuto azionare la leva alle 8:20? (non è un spoiler, okay?!). Si domanda come, con l'IQ di una peluria egli possa vestirsene da solo o perché non si fosse mai trovato davanti a un autobus anni addietro. Toler stesso viene dato più spazio che di solito (un plus) e i veri applausi vanno al progettista Thomas Little e al direttore della fotografia Virgil Miller, che hanno creato una vera atmosfera spaventosa... ma questa entrata ha meno logica di un film dei Ritz Brothers. Sono ancora stupefatti su come un pallino possa essere usato come fucile a vento."
}
```

json_output:
{
  "review": "I'm surprised over the number of folks that have rated this entry as their favorite 'Chan'. Didn't they ever see 'Chan at the Opera' or 'Chan at Treasure Island?'--- the latter ironically written by John Larkin, who dropped the ball here. This plot is a train wreck and overloaded with pointless characters. First, viewers are required to recall the sordid details of Steve McBirney's (played by venerable thug and HUAC squealer Marc Lawrence) 1929's murder spree. Let's not forget he escaped a capital murder rap at the courthouse with a lone policeman on his tail. There's also a victim that was fished out of a river 11 years earlier that no one ever seems concerned about. Then there's the suspension of disbelief required when all the characters are seemingly trapped in the wax museum (although Inspector O'Matthews manages to wield his fat wet rear end inside through a window). Why is Joan Valerie (as Cream's assistant) in this movie? She can't even handle pliers properly--- I realize Chan suffers the same boo-boo but yeesh, he's 66 years old here-- (and has less than 10 lines--- and her character's motivation is too weak to ever be adequately 'splained (excuse me, when I'm on a rant I write like Ricky Ricardo). The Mary Bolton (Marguerite Chapman) character is written to as a eager wide-eyed moron, apparently existing only for the vapid romantic interest of horndog lawyer Tom Agnew (played by the ferret-faced Ted Osborne). Why is Willie Fern a character? Why couldn't McBirney's henchman pulled the switch at 8:20? (not a spoiler, okay?!). One wonders how, with the IQ of lint he manages to dress himself or why he hadn't stepped in front of a bus years ago. Toler himself is given a little more acting rope than usual (a plus) and the real kudos go to set designer Thomas Little and cinematographer Virgil Miller who created some genuinely spooky atmosphere... but this entry has less logic than a Ritz Brothers film. I'm still boggled by how a toothpick can be used as a blow gun.",
