output:
 {
  "review": "The Broadway musical, 'A Chorus Line' is arguably the best musical in theatre. It's about the experiences of people who live for dance; the joys they experience, and the sacrifices they make. Each dancer is auditioning for parts in a Broadway chorus line, yet what comes out of each of them are stories of how their lives led them find dance as a respite. <br /><br />The film version, though, captures none of the passion or beauty of the stage show, and is arguably the worst film adaptation of a Broadway musical, as it is lifeless and devoid of any affection for dance, whatsoever. <br /><br />The biggest mistake was made in giving the director's job to Sir Richard Attenborough, whose direction offered just the right touch and pacing for 'Gandhi.' Why would anyone in his or her right mind ask an epic director to direct a musical that takes place in a fairly constricted place?<br /><br />Which brings us to the next problem. 'A Chorus Line' takes place on stage in a theatre with no real sets and limited costume changes. It's the least flashy of Broadway musicals, and its simplicity was its glory. However, that doesn't translate well to film, and no one really thought that it would. For that reason, the movie should have taken us in the lives of these dancers, and should have left the theatre and audition process. The singers could have offered their songs in other environments and even have offered flashbacks to their first ballet, jazz or tap class. Heck, they could have danced down Broadway in their lively imaginations. Yet, not one shred of imagination went into the making of this film, as Attenborough's complete indifference for dance and the show itself is evident in his lackadaisical direction.<br /><br />Many scenes are downright awkward as the dancers tell their story to the director (Michael Douglas) whether he wants to hear them or not. Douglas' character is capricious about choosing to whom he extends a sympathetic ear, and to whom he has no patience. <br /><br />While the filmmakers pretended to be true to the nature of the play, some heretical changes were made. The very beautiful 'Hello Twelve, Hello Thirteen, Hello Love'--a smashing stage number which took the dancers back to their adolescence--was removed and replaced with the dreadful, 'Surprise,' a song so bad that it was nominated for an Oscar. Adding insult to injury, 'Surprise' simply retold the same story as 'Hello, Love' but without the wit or pathos.<br /><br />There is no reason to see this film unless you want a lesson in what NOT to do when transferring a Broadway show to film. If you want to see a film version of this show, the next closest thing is Bob Fosse's brilliant 'All That Jazz.' While Fosse's daughter is in 'A Chorus Line,' HE is the Fosse who should have been involved, as director. He would have known what to do with this material, which deserved far greater respect than this sad effort.",
  "translate": "Il musical di Broadway, 'A Chorus Line', è probabilmente il miglior musical del teatro. Racconta le esperienze di persone che vivono per la danza; i gioielli che essi provano e i sacrifici che fanno. Ogni ballerino sta auditionando per parti in una linea da coro di Broadway, ma ciò che esce da ciascuno di loro sono storie sulla vita che li hanno portati a trovare la danza come un rifugio. <br /><br />La versione cinematografica, invece, non cattura nessuna passione o bellezza dello spettacolo teatrale originale e è probabilmente la peggiore adattazione cinematografica di un musical di Broadway, visto che è priva di vita e devoida di qualsiasi affetto per la danza, in ogni caso. <br /><br />Il più grande sbaglio è stato fatto assegnando il lavoro del regista a Sir Richard Attenborough, la cui direzione offriva appena il tocco giusto e il ritmo per 'Gandhi.' Perché qualcuno in suo diritto mente avrebbe chiesto a un regista epico a dirigere un musical che si svolge in un posto relativamente limitato?<br /><br /> Ciò ci porta alla prossima problema. 'A Chorus Line' si svolge sul palcoscenico di un teatro senza veri e propri set e cambiamenti di costumi limitati. È il meno flashy di Broadway musicals, e la sua semplicità era la sua gloria. Tuttavia, ciò non traduce bene al cinema, e nessuno realmente ha pensato che sarebbe successo. Per questo motivo, il film dovrebbe aver preso noi nelle vite di questi ballerini e dovrebbe aver lasciato il teatro e il processo di audizione. Gli interpreti potevano offrire le loro canzoni in altri ambienti e addirittura offrire flashback alla sua prima classe di balletto, jazz o tap. Per favore, potrebbero aver ballato per Broadway nelle loro vivaci immagini mentali. Tuttavia, nessuna scintilla di immaginazione è andata nel fare del film, come l'indifferenza di Attenborough per la danza e lo spettacolo stesso è evidente nella sua direzione debole.<br /><br />Molte scene sono davvero umilianti, poiché i ballerini raccontano la loro storia al regista (Michael Douglas) se li vogliono ascoltare o no. Il personaggio di Douglas è capriccioso nel concedere un ascolto simpatetico a chiunque e non ha pazienza per alcuno.<br /><br /> Mentre i produttori cinematografici si sono presi gioco di essere fedeli alla natura dello spettacolo, sono stati fatti qualche cambio eretico. La bellissima 'Hello Twelve, Hello Thirteen, Hello Love'--un numero di scena che riporta i ballerini all'adolescenza--è stata rimossa e sostituita con la terribile 'Surprise,' una canzone così cattiva da essere nominata per un Oscar. Aggiungere insulte alle ferite, 'Surprise' semplicemente ripercorre lo stesso racconto di 'Hello, Love' ma senza il umorismo o patetico.<br /><br /> Non c'è motivo per cui guardare questo film se non vuoi un insegnamento su ciò che NON fare quando si trasferisce uno spettacolo da Broadway al cinema. Se vuoi vedere una versione cinematografica di questa opera, la più vicina cosa è Bob Fosse's brillante 'All That Jazz.' Mentre la figlia di Fosse è in 'A Chorus Line,' LUI è il Fosse che dovrebbe essere coinvolto, come regista. Egli sarebbe stato a conoscenza su cosa fare con questo materiale, che meritava molto più rispetto di questa sforzata sforza."
}

json_output:
{
  "review": "The Broadway musical, 'A Chorus Line' is arguably the best musical in theatre. It's about the experiences of people who live for dance; the joys they experience, and the sacrifices they make. Each dancer is auditioning for parts in a Broadway chorus line, yet what comes out of each of them are stories of how their lives led them find dance as a respite. <br /><br />The film version, though, captures none of the passion or beauty of the stage show, and is arguably the worst film adaptation of a Broadway musical, as it is lifeless and devoid of any affection for dance, whatsoever. <br /><br />The biggest mistake was made in giving the director's job to Sir Richard Attenborough, whose direction offered just the right touch and pacing for 'Gandhi.' Why would anyone in his or her right mind ask an epic director to direct a musical that takes place in a fairly constricted place?<br /><br />Which brings us to the next problem. 'A Chorus Line' takes place on stage in a theatre with no real sets and limited costume changes. It's the least flashy of Broadway musicals, and its simplicity was its glory. However, that doesn't translate well to film, and no one really thought that it would. For that reason, the movie should have taken us in the lives of these dancers, and should have left the theatre and audition process. The singers could have offered their songs in other environments and even have offered flashbacks to their first ballet, jazz or tap class. Heck, they could have danced down Broadway in their lively imaginations. Yet, not one shred of imagination went into the making of this film, as Attenborough's complete indifference for dance and the show itself is evident in his lackadaisical direction.<br /><br />Many scenes are downright awkward as the dancers tell their story to the director (Michael Douglas) whether he wants to hear them or not. Douglas' character is capricious about choosing to whom he extends a sympathetic ear, and to whom he has no patience. <br /><br />While the filmmakers pretended to be true to the nature of the play, some heretical changes were made. The very beautiful 'Hello Twelve, Hello Thirteen, Hello Love'--a smashing stage number which took the dancers back to their adolescence--was removed and replaced with the dreadful, 'Surprise,' a song so bad that it was nominated for an Oscar. Adding insult to injury, 'Surprise' simply retold the same story as 'Hello, Love' but without the wit or pathos.<br /><br />There is no reason to see this film unless you want a lesson in what NOT to do when transferring a Broadway show to film. If you want to see a film version of this show, the next closest thing is Bob Fosse's brilliant 'All That Jazz.' While Fosse's daughter is in 'A Chorus Line,' HE is the Fosse who should have been involved, as director. He would have known what to do with this material, which deserved far greater respect than this sad effort.",
